Diet composition by fish origin
We split species into three groups, reusing the same survey-membership logic as the venn diagram above: freshwater (found only in river), marine (found only in nurse/pomet), and migratory (found in both). For each group, we look at the average diet composition across resource categories (from the literature-curated diet table, not the constructed food webs), normalised so each group’s bars sum to 1.


Code
species_origin &lt;- size |&gt;
  distinct(species_valid, survey) |&gt;
  group_by(species_valid) |&gt;
  summarise(
    surveys = paste(sort(unique(survey)), collapse = ","), .groups = "drop"
  ) |&gt;
  mutate(
    has_river = grepl("river", surveys),
    has_marine = grepl("nurse|pomet", surveys),
    origin = case_when(
      has_river & !has_marine ~ "freshwater",
      !has_river & has_marine ~ "marine",
      has_river & has_marine ~ "migratory"
    )
  )

species_diet &lt;- diet |&gt;
  select(species, all_of(resource_list)) |&gt;
  group_by(species) |&gt;
  summarise(across(everything(), mean), .groups = "drop") |&gt;
  inner_join(
    species_origin |&gt; select(species_valid, origin),
    by = c("species" = "species_valid")
  )

origin_diet &lt;- species_diet |&gt;
  group_by(origin) |&gt;
  summarise(across(all_of(resource_list), mean), .groups = "drop") |&gt;
  tidyr::pivot_longer(
    -origin, names_to = "resource_cat", values_to = "value"
  ) |&gt;
  group_by(origin) |&gt;
  mutate(share = value / sum(value)) |&gt;
  ungroup()

ggplot(
  origin_diet,
  aes(x = factor(origin, levels = c("freshwater", "migratory", "marine")),
      y = share, fill = resource_cat)
) +
  geom_col(width = 0.6) +
  scale_fill_brewer(palette = "Set3") +
  labs(x = NULL, y = "Share of diet (resource categories)", fill = "Resource")









Freshwater and migratory species share a similar diet profile, both with a substantial insect component; marine species show almost no insect consumption (aquatic insects being a specifically freshwater resource) and rely more on zooplankton and phytoplankton instead.

Collinearity between environmental variables
Before fitting statistical models, we check whether environmental variables are correlated with each other, which would make it hard to separate their individual effects. We subsample points to keep the pair plot readable and fast to render.


Code
