Demersal/pelagic position by survey
Same idea, but for demers_pelag and grouped directly by survey rather than by the freshwater/marine/migratory origin above: for each survey, the share of distinct species falling into each demersal/pelagic category.


Code
species_survey &lt;- size |&gt;
  distinct(species_valid, survey)

survey_demers_pelag &lt;- species_survey |&gt;
  left_join(
    species_traits |&gt; select(species, demers_pelag),
    by = c("species_valid" = "species")
  ) |&gt;
  count(survey, demers_pelag) |&gt;
  group_by(survey) |&gt;
  mutate(share = n / sum(n)) |&gt;
  ungroup()

ggplot(survey_demers_pelag, aes(x = survey, y = share, fill = demers_pelag)) +
  geom_col(width = 0.6) +
  scale_fill_brewer(palette = "Set3") +
  labs(x = NULL, y = "Share of species", fill = "Position")









nurse and pomet look alike: both dominated by demersal species (~60%), with the rest split across pelagic-neritic and reef-associated. river is the opposite - mostly benthopelagic (61%) and demersal (31%), with almost no pelagic-neritic and, unsurprisingly, no reef-associated or bathydemersal species at all.

Response distributions
Let’s see the distributions of our response variable so we can choose the relevant reponse distributions.


Code
river_data |&gt;
  select(log_species_richness, connectance, trophic_length, diet_overlap) |&gt;
  tidyr::pivot_longer(everything(), names_to = "metric", values_to = "value") |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(value)) +
  geom_histogram(bins = 40, fill = "steelblue") +
  facet_wrap(~metric, scales = "free") +
  labs(x = NULL, y = NULL)









Connectance and diet overlap are bounded between 0 and 1; they will be properly modeled by a beta distribution. Log species richness might be handle well by a gaussian distribution. The real thinking should be done for the trophic level which is clearly multimodal. Let’s try to investigate why.
We split each local food web by the predation depth of its top species: does it eat no fish at all, a fish that itself only eats invertebrates, or a fish that is itself piscivorous.


Code
river_foodweb &lt;- tar_read(river_foodweb_structure) |&gt;
  filter(operation_id %in% river_data$operation_id)
resource_list &lt;- tar_read(resource_list)

classify_predation_depth &lt;- function(web, resource_list) {
  nodes &lt;- data.frame(node = colnames(web))
  links &lt;- web |&gt;
    as.data.frame() |&gt;
    mutate(resource = rownames(web)) |&gt;
    tidyr::pivot_longer(-resource, names_to = "consumer") |&gt;
    filter(value == 1) |&gt;
    select(-value)
  community &lt;- cheddar::Community(
    nodes = nodes, properties = list(title = "Community"), trophic.links = links
  )
  tl &lt;- cheddar::TrophicLevels(community) |&gt;
    as.data.frame() |&gt;
    tibble::rownames_to_column("node") |&gt;
    rename(TL = ChainAveragedTL)
  top_nodes &lt;- tl$node[!is.na(tl$TL) & tl$TL == max(tl$TL, na.rm = TRUE)]

  fish_prey_of &lt;- function(n) {
    prey &lt;- rownames(web)[web[, n] == 1]
    prey[!(prey %in% resource_list)]
  }
  top_fish_prey &lt;- unique(unlist(lapply(top_nodes, fish_prey_of)))

  if (length(top_fish_prey) == 0) {
    "no fish eaten"
  } else if (any(sapply(top_fish_prey, \(p) length(fish_prey_of(p)) &gt; 0))) {
    "eats a piscivorous fish"
  } else {
    "eats a non-piscivorous fish"
  }
}

river_foodweb &lt;- river_foodweb |&gt;
  mutate(
    predation_depth = sapply(
      foodweb, classify_predation_depth, resource_list = resource_list
    ),
    predation_depth = factor(
      predation_depth,
      levels = c(
        "no fish eaten", "eats a non-piscivorous fish",
        "eats a piscivorous fish"
      )
    )
  )

tl_range &lt;- range(river_foodweb$trophic_length)
normal_overlay &lt;- river_foodweb |&gt;
  group_by(predation_depth) |&gt;
  summarise(
    mean_tl = mean(trophic_length), sd_tl = sd(trophic_length), .groups = "drop"
  ) |&gt;
  rowwise() |&gt;
  reframe(
    predation_depth = predation_depth,
    x = seq(tl_range[1], tl_range[2], length.out = 200),
    y = dnorm(x, mean_tl, sd_tl)
  )

ggplot(river_foodweb, aes(trophic_length)) +
  geom_histogram(
    aes(y = after_stat(density)), bins = 30, fill = "steelblue", alpha = 0.7
  ) +
  geom_line(
    data = normal_overlay, aes(x, y), color = "firebrick", linewidth = 0.8
  ) +
  facet_wrap(~predation_depth, scales = "free") +
  labs(x = "Trophic length", y = "Density")









We see that the ‘predation depth’ cleanly separates our three modes of the trophic level distribution. So we will go for an ordinal model the classify these modes and model each of the mode individually as a gaussian.

