  labs(x = "distance to river mouth", y = "AME (local slope)", color = NULL)









We observe that downstream barrier effect has a strong negative impact on connectance, and this effect vanishes at high distances. By constrast, the barrier upstream has a strong postive impact near the river mouth and this effect vanishes upstream. These patterns are constitent with the hypothesis that first dams from the river mouth trap migratory generalist predator, whose presence increases the connectance locally.
Both credible interactions found for trophic_length point the same direction, and match the same “trapping” hypothesis: dams filter out large, upstream-migrating predators, so they accumulate below the first dam they meet coming from the sea. Taller dams downstream of a site lower its trophic length (harder for predators from the sea to reach it), taller dams upstream raise it (predators blocked from going further accumulate in the reach below, including the site) - and both effects fade with distance to the mouth, consistent with dams mattering most where they sit closest to the sea-derived predator pool.
Still a hypothesis, not a conclusion: the data can’t rule out other differences correlated with tall dams (e.g. altered habitat immediately around them) as the actual driver.
